RULES
- The structure must suit a {{ $json.page_type }}, not a blog post, unless the page type is Blog Post or Guide.
- target_word_count = the recommended_word_count from the competitor analysis (already clamped: service/landing pages 900-2,400, guides 1,200-3,000), or the number the verdict named above when it named one. {{ (($json.competitor_analysis || {}).length_band || {}).ok ? 'LENGTH BAND: ' + $json.competitor_analysis.length_band.basis + '. Plan anywhere inside ' + $json.competitor_analysis.length_band.low + '-' + $json.competitor_analysis.length_band.high + ' words (a number outside it is cut back to ' + $json.competitor_analysis.length_band.target_word_count + ', and the outline with it).' : 'Anything outside 10% of it is cut back to it, and the outline with it, so plan to the number.' }} The outline has 6-9 H2 sections whose target_words add up to this number. Depth beats breadth: fewer sections, each complete.
- Plan a page the writer can finish from the facts above, with no square brackets anywhere in it. A section that could only be written with figures nobody gave (prices, timelines, counts) is the wrong section: plan the qualitative answer instead. Methods, steps and checklists are planned as advice to the reader, never as what the business does unless the facts say so.
- Every item in content_gaps and information_gain_opportunities from the competitor analysis must be covered somewhere in the outline.
- Use question-style H2s where the People Also Ask data shows questions about the same reading of this search; put those questions in snippet_targets with a direct answer. A question about another product, a broader topic or another kind of buyer is not a section of this page.
- Each point has one home: give every claim, definition and list one owning section in the outline (its key_points), and plan no second section, callout or closing line that says it again.
- terms_to_use: 12-20 terms from the shared-terms list that the page must use naturally, with a realistic minimum count. Choose enough of them to reach the coverage target above, and never at the cost of readable sentences: coverage comes from covering the subject, not from repeating words. Coverage is measured the same way on the draft and on the best page: a term counts once it appears. min_count is a writing guide (how often the subject naturally needs the word), never a quota: set it from what the page's length allows, not from the competitor's repetitions.
- Every subtopic in the COVERAGE TARGET block must be answered somewhere in the outline — as its own section or inside a related one, as far as it fits the page being written: the target page was chosen among results of this page's kind, but a heading that sells something, addresses a different audience (for example agencies reselling the service) or names another company is not a subtopic — leave it out. Our own structure, our own words and our own order: never copy that page's headings or sentences (the draft is checked for overlap against every competitor page read, and over 15% of identical 8-word runs fails the quality check).
- evidence_plan may ONLY contain facts from the VERIFIED FACTS list, copied exactly with their URL. Prefer the official, standards, research and major-press sources; use at most one page per vendor or agency blog on the whole page, and never for a market or compliance figure.
- internal_link_suggestions may ONLY use URLs from the REAL PAGES list (3-6 links). Never invent a URL.
- Do not copy competitor headings. Build a better, more complete structure.
- experience_notes: never invent an experience, client, number or result. Experience is what separates this page from AI-written competitors: tie each note to a real fact when one exists.
- A HUB PAGE, CASE STUDY or LOCAL PAGE block above overrides the section-count, length and internal-link-count rules where they conflict.
- Write in {{ $json.language_name || 'English' }} with {{ $json.spelling }} spelling.

prompts.Copywriter:
