{{ JSON.stringify({ h1: $json.content_brief.h1, meta_title: $json.content_brief.meta_title, meta_description: $json.content_brief.meta_description, slug: $json.content_brief.slug, primary_keyword: $json.content_brief.primary_keyword, secondary_keywords: $json.content_brief.secondary_keywords, answer_block: $json.content_brief.answer_block, outline: $json.content_brief.outline, comparison_table: $json.content_brief.comparison_table, image_suggestions: $json.content_brief.image_suggestions, faqs: $json.content_brief.faqs, cta: $json.content_brief.cta, target_word_count: $json.content_brief.target_word_count, proof_elements: $json.content_brief.proof_elements }, null, 1) }}

ALLOWED INTERNAL LINKS (use ONLY these URLs, exactly as written; if the list is empty, write no internal links)
{{ JSON.stringify(($json.content_brief || {}).internal_link_suggestions || [], null, 1) }}

OUTPUT FORMAT (Markdown, in this exact order)
Title: <meta_title, max 60 characters>
Meta Description: <meta_description, 120-155 characters>
Slug: <slug>
Primary Keyword: <primary_keyword>
Secondary Keywords: <comma separated>

# <h1>

<Introduction of 60-90 words: the primary keyword in the first sentence, the specific outcome the reader gets, no throat-clearing>

**Quick answer:** <the answer_block, 40-70 words, one paragraph, no placeholders>

Then every H2 from the outline, in order ("## " for H2, "### " for H3), each covering all its key points in its stated format and close to its target_words. Where the brief suggests an image, add a line: [Image: <alt text>]. Include the comparison table as a Markdown table in the most relevant section; compare what the reader can check (what each option covers, what it suits, what it needs from them), never a price or a timeline the facts do not state. Place the internal links naturally as [anchor](url).
A question from the brief's "faqs" list gets its own question-style heading ("### <the question>") inside the section it belongs to when its answer says something that section does not already say: answer it in the first 40-60 words under the heading, then the detail. A question that is the main search in other words may keep its heading, answered in one or two new sentences plus what is specific to that wording, never the same sentences again. When the section already answers a question in full, give it no heading of its own. Do NOT write a "Frequently Asked Questions" block, a Q1:/A1: list or any FAQ markup: Google stopped showing FAQ rich results in May 2026, while AI assistants quote a question heading with a direct answer under it.
The CTA section is always LAST and uses the brief's cta.
Aim for {{ $json.content_brief.target_word_count }} words of main content; the length is judged as the band in THE PAGE CONTRACT above.

WRITING RULES — these decide the quality
1. Specific beats general. Every section contains at least two of: a concrete example, a number with its linked source, a numbered step list, a decision rule ("choose X if…"), a mistake to avoid with its consequence, or a client fact.
2. No filler. Never use: "in today's fast-paced world", "ever-evolving landscape", "delve", "dive into", "unlock", "unleash", "seamless", "seamlessly", "robust", "leverage", "cutting-edge", "game-changer", "game-changing", "revolutionize", "elevate", "empower", "navigate the complexities", "look no further", "it's important to note", "at the end of the day", "in conclusion", "comprehensive solution", "tailored solutions", "harness the power", "whether you're a", "a testament to", "crucial", "paramount", "realm", "tapestry", "embark", "foster", "streamline", "synergy", "holistic", "best-in-class", "world-class", "state-of-the-art", "top-notch", "one-stop shop", "in this article", "without further ado", "unparalleled", "transformative", "stay ahead of the curve", "when it comes to", "a wide range of".
3. Voice: talk to the reader as "you", active voice, plain words, sentences of 8-22 words with variety, paragraphs of 1-4 sentences, no rhetorical questions in body text, no exclamation marks, no bold inside paragraphs except the Quick answer label.
4. Facts: state a number only if it is in the verified facts or client facts, then link its source. Where a business-specific number (price, timeline, count, award, review, guarantee) is missing, rewrite the sentence so it does not need one: what drives the cost, which choices change the timeline, what to ask a vendor for. Never write a square-bracket placeholder or note of any kind ([Price], [Timeline], [Confirm: ...], [Author insight: ...]): a page with a bracket in it cannot be published. Never write the word "placeholder".
5. Headings are descriptive and scannable, use the snippet-target questions as headings where given, and never copy competitor headings.
5b. Every sentence is written from scratch. The draft is compared with every competitor page that was read and with this site's own pages: more than 15% of it matching one page in runs of eight words or more fails the quality check and comes back for a rewrite. Cover the same ground where the brief asks for it, in our own words, order and examples — never reuse a competitor's sentences, lists, table rows or definitions, and never repeat passages of another page of this website.
6. Use the primary keyword in the H1, the first sentence and the final section. An H2 may carry it where a reader would phrase the heading that way, never forced. Otherwise write naturally and never stuff.
6b. Each point has one home: explain it fully once, in the section that owns it. The Quick answer sums up the page and a question heading that asks the main search in other words answers it briefly; every other section refers back in a few words or links to it, and never restates it. No callout box, closing line or call to action repeats a point the page has already made.
7. Show expertise: explain the why behind each recommendation, name the trade-offs, and give the reader a way to decide. Give each information-gain item its own full treatment.
8. No schema, script or HTML. No production notes, layout notes, image briefs or commentary anywhere: after the Secondary Keywords line the next line is the H1. Return only the Markdown, starting with the Title line.
9. Length: stay inside the band in THE PAGE CONTRACT. Finish every section and the CTA; if space is tight, shorten sections rather than dropping the ending.
10. Sources: every external number is quoted exactly as its source writes it and carries that source's link. Prefer an official, government, standards or major research / press source; a vendor or agency blog may be used at most once on the whole page. Never add tracking parameters (utm_*, srsltid, gclid, fbclid) to a link. A number you cannot find in the verified facts above does not go on the page. A market, adoption, cost or compliance figure needs an official, research or major-press source: when its only source is a vendor's or an agency's blog, write the sentence without the number.

