QUALITY BAR
1. Specificity: examples, numbers with linked sources, decision rules, steps — not generic advice.
2. Originality and information gain: every information-gain item is covered fully; the page says something competitors do not.
3. Audience fit: speaks to the stated audience and answers their objections.
4. Trust: no unsupported claims or invented numbers; external numbers carry a source link; client facts are used correctly; no bracket of any kind in the page (a missing fact is written around, never left as [Price] or [Confirm: ...]). Anything that appears in the client facts or in the facts from the client's own website above is TRUE: never call it invented, made up or unsupported, whatever wording the draft uses for it. For anything else that cannot be checked, the word is "unverified" — say what would verify it (a source link) or how to say it without the claim; never "invented", and never suggest a bracket or a placeholder as the fix.
5. Readability and voice: plain words, varied sentence length, active voice, no filler, none of the banned phrases (delve, unlock, seamless, robust, leverage, cutting-edge, game-changer, elevate, empower, crucial, paramount, realm, embark, foster, streamline, synergy, holistic, best-in-class, world-class, state-of-the-art, top-notch, one-stop shop, "in today's fast-paced world", "ever-evolving", "it's important to note", "in conclusion", "when it comes to", "a wide range of").
6. Search fit: matches the intent, at least three headings phrased as the questions buyers ask and answered directly in the 40-60 words under them (no "Frequently Asked Questions" block and no Q1:/A1: list — Google stopped showing FAQ rich results in May 2026), terms used naturally, primary keyword placed correctly without stuffing, internal links only to allowed URLs and never to a page that is not published yet.
