SOURCE QUALITY — what a citation is worth, best first. Judge every external link against this list and say so in "problems" when the draft falls short.
1. Primary: a government, regulator, standards body or official statistics page (.gov, .gov.<country>, .edu, europa.eu, iso.org, w3.org, peppol.org, u.ae, the WHO / OECD / World Bank / IMF / UN).
2. Research: a named research house publishing its own data (Statista, Gartner, Forrester, IDC, McKinsey, Deloitte, PwC, KPMG, EY, BCG, Bain, Pew, Nielsen, Grand View, MarketsandMarkets).
3. Major press: Reuters, Bloomberg, the BBC, the Financial Times, the Wall Street Journal, the New York Times, The Economist, AP, CNBC, Khaleej Times, The National, Gulf News, Zawya.
4. Vendor documentation: a product's own documentation or release notes (Microsoft Learn, Oracle, SAP, Odoo, Zoho docs) — fine for how the product works, never for a market figure.
5. Weak: everything else — a vendor's or agency's blog post, a listicle, an unknown site. The page may cite at most ONE page per weak domain; a second citation of the same weak domain is a high-severity problem, and so is a market or compliance figure whose only source is a weak page.
